# Standardization Purpose #1

## With `typeof` as a Practical Example



## Very simple feature

```cpp
typeof(1) main () {
	return 0;
}
// same as:
int main () {
	return 0;
}
```

- Put in expression, get out type
- First mentioned in C89 rationale (!!)
- Usable in GCC since time immemorial
- Ubiquitous in all ecosystems ...




## E X C E P T .  M I C R O S O F T .  V I S U A L .  C .

```cpp
typeof(1) main () {
	return 0;
}
```

```sh
<source>(1): error C2143: syntax error: missing ')' before 'constant'
<source>(1): error C2143: syntax error: missing '{' before 'constant'
<source>(1): error C2059: syntax error: 'constant'
<source>(1): error C2059: syntax error: ')'
Compiler returned: 2
```

- 35+ year old extension, but Microsoft won't add it
- Every compiler has the technology for it already
- It is 202x -- why won't Microsoft implement this?!



## It's Not Standard?!

One of those very old things that were not standardized, so...

- N2927 put it into ISO C in 2022
- Rolled out with the C23 Standard



## Suddenly?

Microsoft Visual C has `typeof`, within 1~2 months of ratification of the C Standard

```cpp
typeof(1) main () {
	return 0;
}
```

```sh
Compiler returned: 0
```



## Standardization Purpose #1

Elevate User requests

- 30+ years of ignoring ➡️➡️ 1 month implemented
- Low-stakes, low-cost, high-impact feature
- Improves shared compiler/platform codebase stability

Conformance has TEETH!

![A black cat with their mouth open showing off their sharp teeth and fang-like canines](source/img/yawn-cat.jpg)




# Standardization Purpose #2

### `<threads.h>`: An Aspirational Example



## `<threads.h>` is a bit strange!

First standardized in C11 during the  
"we need mutexes and multithreading stuff" push

- Modeled pretty strongly after POSIX threads (pthreads) capabilities
- Much of it very easily implementable on top of most major platforms



## But it wasn't... implemented?!

January 2024 (13 years later):
- Apple: No
- Windows: No
- Linux (glibc): Yes, but often ignored?
- Other libcs: No lmao



## Lots of Non-Conformance!

Tends to happen with library features vs. language feature  
(At the same time: C++, with similar features, has 100% conformance)



## Reports on Why It's Not A Priority?

- "Lacks power: can't set thread name, affinity, stack sizes, etc."
- "I can just use {Platform-specific wrapper}"
- "I can use a C11 Threads wrapper"



## Only Mild Improvement

October 2026 (15 years, today):
- Apple: No
- Windows: Yes, still kinda ignored
- Linux (glibc): Yes, still kinda ignored
- Other libcs: No lmao



## How To Improve Conformance?

One important criticism:

#### "Lacks power: can't set thread name, affinity, stack sizes, etc."



## How To Improve Conformance?

Add power with a new C2y feature:  
Gives C2y ability to set thread names, stack size, affinities, and more

[![A picture of the current rendition of the Thread Attributes paper](source/img/thread-attr-paper.png)](https://thephd.dev/_vendor/future_cxx/papers/C%20-%20Thread%20Attributes%20-%20Implementation%20Extensible%20and%20ABI-Resistant.html)



## Buy-in !

A lot of implementers (embedded developers and 'Other' libc developers)  
saw this interface...

and liked it!!

![A tweet from the Dreamcast Homebrew SDK KOS Developer](source/img/kos-dev-tweet.png)



## Standardization Purpose #2:

Give Implementers and Users something they like!

- Getting multiple vendors to approve the design
- Users getting more power
- "Carrot" -- attract implementation and user excitement with good features





# Standardization Purpose #3

## The `realloc` Scuffle



## `realloc` was Implementation-Defined / Unspecified up to C17



## C23 -> Undefined Behavior

[![A 2019 paper from Robert Seacord titled Zero-Size Reallocations are Undefined Behavior](source/img/realloc-ub-paper.png)](https://www.open-std.org/JTC1/SC22/WG14/www/docs/n2464.pdf)



## Caused a Stir

Published in ACM:

[![A picture of an article by Terence Kelly called Catch-23: The New C Standard Sets the World on Fire](source/img/catch23-article.png)](https://queue.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3588242)



## But Why?

☢️ D a n g e r o u s ☢️

[![An article from a Singaporean Security Researcher called How a double-free bug in WhatsApp turns to RCE](source/img/whatsapp-bug.png)](https://awakened1712.github.io/hacking/hacking-whatsapp-gif-rce/)



## How?

2 major implementation categories are **mutually negative to one another**

- Program on the first platform/libc category and switch to the second:  
  ➡️ **USE-AFTER FREE BUG!!**
- Program on the second platform/libc category and switch to the first:  
  ➡️ **DOUBLE FREE BUG!!**



## Standardization Purpose #3:

Recognizing reality and matching what happens on the ground

- Has caused lots of motion and discussion in implementations
- Might actually get a less-dangerous specification to overturn UB (!!)
- Will take time, but discussion would not have happened otherwise
